00:00:01.320 --> 00:00:04.150 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  when we think of viruses  we often think of the biological agents  responsible for influenza  ebola or the covid19 pandemic  the damage caused by these viruses on  society is significant  but why do viruses infect us what role  do they have in biology  and is there a way we can harness  viruses to benefit humanity  let's start by looking at what a virus  is  viruses are small infectious agents made  up of genetic material  either dna or rna surrounded by a  protective protein layer called  the capsid the capsid is made up of  identical building blocks known as  that fit together to make the 3d  structure of the virus  these can be classified into  non-enveloped viruses  that can have complex helical or  icosahedral figure  and enveloped viruses which are  spherical  as a capsid is surrounded by a membrane  unlike most other biological agents  viruses can't reproduce  on their own instead they infect a host  cell  hijack the cell's machinery and force it  to produce new viruses  the type of virus determines how it gets  inside a cell  some non-enveloped viruses have proteins  on their surface  that bind to receptors on the outside of  cells  think of the viral surface protein as a  key that fits into the lock on the  cell's surface  if the protein fits the cell is unlocked  and the virus can enter  other non-enveloped viruses attach to  the outside of the cell  and essentially poke a needle through  the membrane to deposit viral genetic  material inside  enveloped viruses on the other hand are  surrounded by a membrane like the one on  cells  this allows the virus to fuse with the  cell releasing the capsid inside  regardless of the strategy viruses use  to enter the cell  the process that follows is the same the  viral genetic  material ends up at the ribosomes the  cellular machinery  that will translate the viral genetic  material into proteins  these come together to form new viruses  which can leave the cell  either by pushing through the membrane  or by lysing it  which can eventually lead to cell death  the human body has natural defenses  against viruses  such as immune cells that attack them  and antibodies that bind to them  after a virus is successfully eliminated  the immune system retains a memory of  the pathogen  this way the next time the same virus  infects us  our bodies can respond faster and more  efficiently  unfortunately our bodies are not always  capable of eliminating the virus  in this case the best way to protect  ourselves is through vaccines  a form of artificial immunity that  involves administering  low doses of weakened viruses to the  body  this mimics the process of natural  immunity allowing the body to generate a  memory of the virus  without the vaccinated individual  actually developing the disease  when no vaccine or treatment exists and  the body cannot mount a sufficient  attack  on its own we are at risk of the virus  replicating out of control  and spreading throughout the population  a prime example of this  is the covid19 pandemic caused by the  novel coronavirus  sars kov2 this virus is thought to be  zoonotic  meaning it was first transmitted from  animals to humans  the first outbreak was recorded in wuhan  china in december of 2019  and the virus quickly spread across the  globe infecting over 20 million people  and killing over 820 000 as of august  2020 despite the harm that viruses can  cause most of them don't actually infect  humans  in fact some are critical to our  survival bacteriophages for instance  are complex viruses that help us fight  off harmful bacteria they hijack the  bacteria that are infecting the body  and force them to halt the production of  harmful bacterial components  stopping the damage that the bacteria  were causing  since bacteriophages do not damage our  cells in the process  they have been used to fight bacterial  infections like e coli  and staph as bacteria become more  resistant to antibiotics  antibiotics can fail in protecting us  which is where  bacteriophages can become especially  useful  with new technology we are learning how  to harness viruses  to use them for our benefit nowadays we  treat some as  messenger vessels by removing the  potentially dangerous genes viruses  would insert  to our dna and replacing them with  sequences  that can be useful for us for example  the herpes virus has been used to treat  cancer  by replacing the genetic material of the  virus with new dna  that can target and attack tumor cells  although there's still a lot to be  learned about viruses  it is becoming clear that they can be  more than just the diseases they are  known to cause  they might even become the next  superheroes of medicine  [Music]  you

